<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Île de la Cité</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Île de la Cité is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the oldest core of the city. It is thought to have formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a>, the town of the Parisii, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held the royal palace and the cathedral of Notre-Dame, begun in 1163. The Pillar of the Boatmen, a Gallo-Roman monument dedicated to Jupiter under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, was found beneath the cathedral in 1711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite.jpg" alt="Île de la Cité" width="320" height="113" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DXR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%8Ele_de_la_Cit%C3%A9_shortly_before_sunrise,_West_View_140320_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Île de la Cité is one of two natural islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the other being Île Saint-Louis. It covers 22.5 hectares. Bridges once ran on both sides of it, and it was the most convenient place to cross the river. The Parisii, a small Gallic tribe and known river traders, lived nearby, and their coins of about 100 BCE have been found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. A Gallic settlement called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a> may have stood on the island since the 3rd century BCE, though no significant traces earlier than the 1st century CE have been found there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 4th century the island held the fortress of the Roman governor of the area. In 508 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, the first king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, set up his castle there, and an early cathedral, Saint-Étienne, was built in the Romanesque style. In 1160 Bishop Maurice de Sully began a new Gothic cathedral, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris</a>, whose first stones were laid by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> in 1163. It was largely complete by 1345. Louis IX built the Sainte-Chapelle to house relics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-thorns">Crown of Thorns</a>, which he bought in 1239 from the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; it was consecrated in 1248.</p><p>Crowding and bad conditions led the kings to leave. After the Parisian rebellion of 1358 led by Étienne Marcel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> moved first to the Hôtel Saint-Pol, then to the Bastille and finally to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. The island remained a centre of justice, and in 1302 the first meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> was held in the old palace. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the Conciergerie held Marie-Antoinette, who was sentenced to the guillotine in 1793, and later Robespierre, arrested there on 27 July 1794.</p><p>In the mid-19th century, after cholera epidemics in 1832 and 1849, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> ordered the old streets pulled down and wide avenues opened; in 1867 he demolished the medieval Hôtel-Dieu and replaced it with the present hospital. Henry IV had built the Pont Neuf, finished in 1606, and the triangular Place Dauphine from 1609.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Notre-Dame stands at the eastern end with the Place Jean-Paul-II before it. Its spire and much of the roof were destroyed by fire on 15 April 2019, and the cathedral reopened on 7 December 2024. The Sainte-Chapelle, the Conciergerie and the Palais de Justice lie along the Quai de l'Horloge, and the Préfecture de Police, the Tribunal de commerce and the Hôtel-Dieu, reputed to be the oldest functioning hospital in the world, are also on the island. The Mémorial des Martyrs de la Déportation, dedicated in 1962, commemorates the 200,000 people deported to Nazi camps from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Île de la Cité” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is believed that before the arrival of Christianity in France a Gallo-Roman temple dedicated to Jupiter stood on the site of Notre-Dame, on the <b>Île de la Cité</b>, an island in the Seine.</blockquote><a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome Lutetia was rebuilt on the Montagne Sainte-Geneviève, and on the <b>Île de la Cité</b> the boatmen dedicated the Pillar of the Boatmen to Jupiter under Tiberius.</blockquote><a href="/state/parisii">Parisii</a></li></ul></section>
